1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,098
This is your full short track race day routine, from race week to the start line.

2
00:00:05,098 --> 00:00:10,385
You will get a simple order to follow, a packing list, and a checklist you can copy.

3
00:00:10,385 --> 00:00:12,588
Here is the whole plan in one line.

4
00:00:12,588 --> 00:00:15,735
Sharpen and settle your blades early in race week.

5
00:00:15,735 --> 00:00:18,379
Pack from a written list the night before.

6
00:00:18,379 --> 00:00:24,799
Then spend the final hour warming up off the ice and putting your kit on in the same order every time.

7
00:00:24,799 --> 00:00:29,142
Get those three things right, and the rest of the day is just racing.

8
00:00:29,842 --> 00:00:35,419
Ask experienced skaters where finals are won and lost, and very few point at the ice.

9
00:00:35,419 --> 00:00:40,800
They point at the changing room, the bag, and the last hour before the first heat.

10
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:44,277
Short track margins are tiny and the contact is real.

11
00:00:44,277 --> 00:00:50,117
A blade that has lost its edge will wash out on the apex, right when you load it hardest.

12
00:00:50,117 --> 00:00:54,710
A bolt that backed off by half a turn lets your blade shift under you.

13
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:57,663
A cold hip flexor cramps in the first corner.

14
00:00:57,663 --> 00:00:59,894
None of these are talent problems.

15
00:00:59,894 --> 00:01:05,077
They are preparation problems, and every one of them is fixable the day before.

16
00:01:05,077 --> 00:01:10,523
The skaters who podium again and again treat their gear checks as part of the race.

17
00:01:11,223 --> 00:01:12,754
Race week has one rule.

18
00:01:12,754 --> 00:01:15,747
Change nothing that your body has to relearn.

19
00:01:15,747 --> 00:01:21,934
Your feet and your balance have spent weeks adapting to your boot, your bend and your rocker.

20
00:01:21,934 --> 00:01:25,127
Sharpen early in the week, not the night before.

21
00:01:25,127 --> 00:01:31,314
A fresh grind can feel grabby, so skate at least one session on the new edge before you race.

22
00:01:31,314 --> 00:01:34,374
Deburr fully so no wire edge drags on the ice.

23
00:01:34,374 --> 00:01:38,432
If your setup already feels right, leave it exactly as it is.

24
00:01:38,432 --> 00:01:45,350
Write your alignment down, or take a photo of the cup position, so you can return to a known good setup.

25
00:01:45,350 --> 00:01:48,743
Then walk through every bolt on your mounting cups.

26
00:01:48,743 --> 00:01:51,337
Cups can loosen slowly under side load.

27
00:01:51,337 --> 00:01:58,921
If you run adapters or spacers, check they sit square, because a twisted blade feels like a vague, wandering push.

28
00:01:59,621 --> 00:02:05,099
This timeline splits race day into three windows, each with its own short job list.

29
00:02:05,099 --> 00:02:12,755
In race week, you sharpen and deburr early, check every mount bolt, skate the new edge once, and change nothing new.

30
00:02:12,755 --> 00:02:21,268
The night before, you pack the full bag, fit your guards and soakers, lay out your race kit, and then sleep instead of tinkering.

31
00:02:21,268 --> 00:02:30,442
In the hour before, you warm up off the ice, wipe your edges, fit your gloves, helmet and neck guard, and take your guards off at the line.

32
00:02:30,442 --> 00:02:32,158
Nothing is left to chance.

33
00:02:32,858 --> 00:02:36,632
Packing from memory is how skaters arrive with one glove.

34
00:02:36,632 --> 00:02:40,207
Build a written list once and reuse it for every meet.

35
00:02:40,207 --> 00:02:49,277
Your bag holds your boots with blades mounted, hard guards for walking around the venue, and soft soakers to pull moisture off the steel.

36
00:02:49,277 --> 00:02:56,427
Add your race suit, a spare base layer, your safety kit, a cloth for the edges, tape, water and simple food.

37
00:02:56,427 --> 00:02:59,605
Then keep a small tool roll in the bag for good.

38
00:02:59,605 --> 00:03:04,371
Put in the driver that fits your cup bolts and two or three spare bolts.

39
00:03:04,371 --> 00:03:10,131
Add a spare set of laces, a spare pair of glove tips, and a small diamond deburr stone.

40
00:03:10,131 --> 00:03:14,831
All of it fits in a space smaller than a paperback, and it saves meets.

41
00:03:15,531 --> 00:03:22,058
Pack in the order you will use things, so the first thing you need is the first thing your hand finds.

42
00:03:22,058 --> 00:03:23,978
The top layer comes out first.

43
00:03:23,978 --> 00:03:30,568
That is your helmet, neck guard, undersuit, socks and gloves, because you put your safety kit on first.

44
00:03:30,568 --> 00:03:37,095
The middle layer is what you use at the boards: boots with blades, guards, soakers and your race suit.

45
00:03:37,095 --> 00:03:44,581
The bottom layer is the one you hope not to need: the tool roll, spare bolts, laces, glove tips and the deburr stone.

46
00:03:44,581 --> 00:03:51,108
When you are three heats deep and tired, a bag you can read at a glance saves you from silly mistakes.

47
00:03:51,807 --> 00:03:55,297
Aim to arrive ninety minutes before your first heat.

48
00:03:55,297 --> 00:03:59,927
That covers a queue at the sharpening bench and a busy changing room.

49
00:03:59,927 --> 00:04:02,477
Then follow the same order every time.

50
00:04:02,477 --> 00:04:08,046
Sixty minutes out, a light jog, hip openers, ankle circles and a few gentle lunges.

51
00:04:08,046 --> 00:04:14,421
Forty five minutes out, three or four accelerations of six to eight seconds with full recovery.

52
00:04:14,421 --> 00:04:19,521
Thirty minutes out, undersuit, socks and race suit on, then your neck guard.

53
00:04:19,521 --> 00:04:23,278
Twenty minutes out, boots on, laces set, helmet buckled.

54
00:04:23,278 --> 00:04:28,915
Ten minutes out, gloves and tips on, wipe the edges dry, and guards on for the walk.

55
00:04:28,915 --> 00:04:36,565
Use the official warm up for easy laps and a corner entry or two at pace, then get straight back into warm layers.

56
00:04:36,565 --> 00:04:39,048
At the line, guards off, and breathe.

57
00:04:39,747 --> 00:04:43,341
A short track edge grips through its two crisp corners.

58
00:04:43,341 --> 00:04:46,412
On the left, a sharp square edge bites the ice.

59
00:04:46,412 --> 00:04:53,533
On the right, those corners have rounded off, and the blade slides on the apex just when you load it hardest.

60
00:04:53,533 --> 00:04:55,363
So protect the edge all day.

61
00:04:55,363 --> 00:05:02,158
Wipe the blades with a dry cloth before every heat, because rink ice leaves a film of moisture and grit.

62
00:05:02,158 --> 00:05:08,168
Between heats, dry the steel again and slide the soakers on the moment you step off the ice.

63
00:05:08,168 --> 00:05:11,370
In a humid rink, standing water starts rust fast.

64
00:05:11,370 --> 00:05:14,833
And never walk on bare blades on matting or concrete.

65
00:05:14,833 --> 00:05:18,296
One hard step can nick an edge and cost you the meet.

66
00:05:18,995 --> 00:05:24,745
Short track is a contact sport on steel edges, so the safety kit is not optional.

67
00:05:24,745 --> 00:05:30,282
This diagram shows how each layer guards a part of the body a blade can reach.

68
00:05:30,282 --> 00:05:31,631
A certified helmet.

69
00:05:31,631 --> 00:05:34,115
A Level 3 cut resistant neck guard.

70
00:05:34,115 --> 00:05:35,322
Gloves with tips.

71
00:05:35,322 --> 00:05:37,238
A Level 3 Kevlar undersuit.

72
00:05:37,238 --> 00:05:45,969
And Level 4 ankle socks, because the ankle sits low, close to other blades, and spends a long time near the ice in a slide.

73
00:05:45,969 --> 00:05:49,661
Add eye protection, shin guards and knee protection.

74
00:05:49,661 --> 00:05:53,778
Wear the whole system for every race, never a partial set.

75
00:05:53,778 --> 00:06:01,231
Federations and meets publish their own equipment rules, and they change, so read them before you travel.

76
00:06:01,231 --> 00:06:04,922
Skaters do get turned away for a missing shin guard.

77
00:06:05,622 --> 00:06:08,596
Sooner or later, something fails between heats.

78
00:06:08,596 --> 00:06:10,368
Decide now what you will do.

79
00:06:10,368 --> 00:06:13,849
If a cup bolt has loosened, stop and do not race on it.

80
00:06:13,849 --> 00:06:18,722
Bring it back to your usual tension, then check the other three on that boot.

81
00:06:18,722 --> 00:06:21,253
If a lace snaps, replace the whole lace.

82
00:06:21,253 --> 00:06:25,746
A knot under the tongue makes a pressure point and lets your heel lift.

83
00:06:25,746 --> 00:06:28,657
If a glove tip tears, swap in your spare pair.

84
00:06:28,657 --> 00:06:34,542
If you nick an edge on matting, take the burr off with your stone and check the edge by feel.

85
00:06:34,542 --> 00:06:40,364
If the damage catches a fingernail across the corner, tell your coach before you race on it.

86
00:06:40,364 --> 00:06:44,414
Each fix takes a couple of minutes if the parts are in your bag.

87
00:06:45,114 --> 00:06:51,214
A meet can mean a heat in the morning and a final in the evening, with long gaps between.

88
00:06:51,214 --> 00:06:53,887
This timeline shows where food belongs.

89
00:06:53,887 --> 00:06:56,423
Eat your main meal before you arrive.

90
00:06:56,423 --> 00:07:04,442
After each race, the green window is for real food, small, plain and familiar, taken soon after you come off the ice.

91
00:07:04,442 --> 00:07:08,075
The orange stretch before each call is for sips only.

92
00:07:08,075 --> 00:07:10,885
Put warm layers on first and food second.

93
00:07:10,885 --> 00:07:15,888
Cold air hides thirst, so carry your own bottle and sip steadily all day.

94
00:07:15,888 --> 00:07:21,097
Try nothing new on race day, and junior skaters should leave caffeine alone.

95
00:07:21,097 --> 00:07:28,910
If you often feel faint, cramp badly or get stomach trouble at meets, speak to a doctor or a registered dietitian.

96
00:07:29,610 --> 00:07:31,843
Here is the one idea to remember.

97
00:07:31,843 --> 00:07:35,496
In race week, change nothing your body has to relearn.

98
00:07:35,496 --> 00:07:43,817
Sharpen early, pack from a written list, warm up in the same order every time, and wear the full safety kit for every race.

99
00:07:43,817 --> 00:07:47,335
Get the preparation right and race day becomes calm.

100
00:07:47,335 --> 00:07:51,935
Your edges bite, your kit is on, and all that is left is the racing.

101
00:07:51,935 --> 00:07:58,768
The full guide, with the complete checklist and answers to common questions, is on the LiveLica blog.

102
00:07:58,768 --> 00:08:00,865
The link is in the description.
